{"id":"activity_oscar_resting_tongue_posture_mewing","name":"Resting Tongue Posture (Mewing)","category":"Recovery","subcategory":"Tongue and swallowing","domain":"grooming","type":"canonical","verb":"Practice","lane_subcategory":"Jaw exercises","is_active":true,"intensity":"low","description":"Resting the tongue lightly against the roof of the mouth with the lips closed and the jaw relaxed.","duration_minutes":2,"default_duration_min":2,"steps":[{"order":1,"title":"Sit or stand tall","instruction":"Sit or stand with your head comfortably aligned."},{"order":2,"title":"Relax the jaw","instruction":"Let the jaw hang loose."},{"order":3,"title":"Close the lips gently","instruction":"Close your lips without pressing them together."},{"order":4,"title":"Place the tongue","instruction":"Rest the tongue tip just behind the upper front teeth, without pushing on them, with the wider tongue resting lightly against the palate."},{"order":5,"title":"Breathe normally","instruction":"Keep breathing quietly through the nose."}],"instructions":"1. Sit or stand with your head comfortably aligned.\n2. Let the jaw hang loose.\n3. Close your lips without pressing them together.\n4. Rest the tongue tip just behind the upper front teeth, without pushing on them, with the wider tongue resting lightly against the palate.\n5. Keep breathing quietly through the nose.","safety_notes":"Keep the pressure very light and the jaw relaxed. Hard pressing can cause jaw-joint soreness and push the front teeth forward. Stop if you get jaw pain or headaches.","contraindications":["Dental work, braces or jaw (TMJ) pain: check with your dentist first","Difficulty swallowing or a choking risk","Oral injury, ulcers or infection","Under 18 unless a clinician has prescribed it"],"benefits":{"primary":"Builds an easy resting tongue and lip posture","secondary":["Relaxed jaw","Nasal-breathing awareness"]},"health_benefits":["Builds an easy resting tongue and lip posture","Relaxed jaw","Nasal-breathing awareness"],"tags":["oscar patel","myofunctional","tongue","mewing","tongue posture"],"body_parts":["mouth","tongue","jaw","lips"],"recommended_duration":"A habit through the day; no timed sets","recommended_frequency":"Daily","recommended_intensity":"Comfortable and pain-free","tips":"Tongue and swallowing exercises taught by a clinician have trial evidence for sleep apnea (systematic review linked); the claim that tongue posture reshapes adult facial bone is not supported by good evidence. Described in Oscar Patel's public materials as compiled on 25 October 2026 from his website, videos, social posts and an unverified community document; the steps are a practical adaptation, not word-for-word instructions.","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC11793941/","is_group":false,"is_prenatal":false,"target_gender":["all"],"target_age_min":18,"sort_order":335},
{"id":"activity_oscar_scalp_mobilization","name":"Scalp Mobilization","category":"Recovery","subcategory":"Self-myofascial release","domain":"fitness","type":"canonical","verb":"Roll","lane_subcategory":"Rest","is_active":true,"intensity":"low","description":"Gentle fingertip movement of the scalp over the skull.","duration_minutes":2,"default_duration_min":2,"steps":[{"order":1,"title":"Spread your fingers","instruction":"Place your fingertips on your scalp."},{"order":2,"title":"Move the scalp","instruction":"Gently move the scalp over the bone in small circles."},{"order":3,"title":"Cover each zone","instruction":"Shift between the forehead, the sides and the crown."}],"instructions":"1. Place your fingertips on your scalp.\n2. Gently move the scalp over the bone in small circles.\n3. Shift between the forehead, the sides and the crown.","safety_notes":"Use light-to-moderate pressure, never directly on a joint or bone. Move slowly and stop if it is sharply painful.","contraindications":["Blood clot or circulation problems, or taking blood thinners, unless your clinician has cleared it","Open wounds, bruising, rash or skin infection at the spot","Sharp, shooting or nerve-type pain (stop; use a lighter touch or skip it)","Scalp infection or open sores"],"benefits":{"primary":"Eases tension in the area before stretching and strengthening","secondary":["Body awareness","Short, easy recovery habit"]},"health_benefits":["Eases tension in the area before stretching and strengthening","Body awareness","Short, easy recovery habit"],"tags":["oscar patel","fascia","self-massage","face"],"body_parts":["face","forehead"],"equipment_needed":["clean hands"],"equipment":["clean hands"],"recommended_duration":"60 seconds","recommended_frequency":"Daily or as needed","recommended_intensity":"Comfortable and pain-free","tips":"Described in Oscar Patel's public materials as compiled on 25 October 2026 from his website, videos, social posts and an unverified community document; the steps are a practical adaptation, not word-for-word instructions.","is_group":false,"is_prenatal":false,"target_gender":["all"],"target_age_min":18,"sort_order":317},
{"id":"activity_oscar_side_neck_isometric_press","name":"Side-Neck Isometric Press","category":"Strength","subcategory":"Neck","domain":"fitness","type":"canonical","verb":"Press","lane_subcategory":"Strength","is_active":true,"intensity":"low","description":"Pressing the side of the head into the hand without moving the neck.","duration_minutes":3,"default_duration_min":3,"steps":[{"order":1,"title":"Sit tall","instruction":"Sit or stand upright."},{"order":2,"title":"Palm to the side","instruction":"Rest one palm against the side of your head."},{"order":3,"title":"Press without moving","instruction":"Press the head gently into the hand without letting the neck move."},{"order":4,"title":"Hold and relax","instruction":"Hold for about 10 seconds, then relax."},{"order":5,"title":"Switch","instruction":"Repeat on the other side."}],"instructions":"1. Sit or stand upright.\n2. Rest one palm against the side of your head.\n3. Press the head gently into the hand without letting the neck move.\n4. Hold for about 10 seconds, then relax.\n5. Repeat on the other side.","safety_notes":"Use gentle effort, no jerking. Do not use weights or hang the head off a bench.","contraindications":["Neck or arm pain, numbness or tingling","Dizziness, visual changes or faintness with neck movement","Recent neck injury or surgery","Known cervical spine or blood-vessel conditions"],"benefits":{"primary":"Strengthens the side-neck muscles with no movement","secondary":["Neck stability"]},"health_benefits":["Strengthens the side-neck muscles with no movement","Neck stability"],"tags":["oscar patel","neck","lateral","isometric"],"body_parts":["neck","cervical_spine","scalenes"],"recommended_duration":"Archive: 3 × 15–30 each side (bench version); beginner form here is a 10-second isometric press","recommended_frequency":"3–4 sessions a week","recommended_intensity":"Comfortable and pain-free","tips":"Low-load deep neck flexor training has trial evidence for neck pain (systematic review linked). Heavier neck loading is not covered here. Described in Oscar Patel's public materials as compiled on 25 October 2026 from his website, videos, social posts and an unverified community document; the steps are a practical adaptation, not word-for-word instructions.","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC6263552/","is_group":false,"is_prenatal":false,"target_gender":["all"],"target_age_min":18,"sort_order":366},
{"id":"activity_oscar_sip_of_water_tongue_placement","name":"Sip-of-Water Tongue Placement","category":"Recovery","subcategory":"Tongue and swallowing","domain":"grooming","type":"canonical","verb":"Practice","lane_subcategory":"Jaw exercises","is_active":true,"intensity":"low","description":"A water sip used to feel the natural position of the tongue after swallowing.","duration_minutes":1,"default_duration_min":1,"steps":[{"order":1,"title":"Take a small sip","instruction":"Take a small sip of water."},{"order":2,"title":"Swallow naturally","instruction":"Swallow normally."},{"order":3,"title":"Notice the tongue","instruction":"Notice where your tongue rests afterwards."},{"order":4,"title":"Relax","instruction":"Let the tongue and jaw soften."}],"instructions":"1. Take a small sip of water.\n2. Swallow normally.\n3. Notice where your tongue rests afterwards.\n4. Let the tongue and jaw soften.","safety_notes":"Sip small amounts and stop if you cough or choke.","contraindications":["Dental work, braces or jaw (TMJ) pain: check with your dentist first","Difficulty swallowing or a choking risk","Oral injury, ulcers or infection","Under 18 unless a clinician has prescribed it"],"benefits":{"primary":"Cue for a natural tongue-rest position","secondary":["Swallowing awareness"]},"health_benefits":["Cue for a natural tongue-rest position","Swallowing awareness"],"tags":["oscar patel","myofunctional","tongue","swallow"],"body_parts":["mouth","tongue","jaw","lips"],"equipment_needed":["water"],"equipment":["water"],"recommended_duration":"Practise as needed","recommended_frequency":"Daily","recommended_intensity":"Comfortable and pain-free","tips":"Tongue and swallowing exercises taught by a clinician have trial evidence for sleep apnea (systematic review linked); the claim that tongue posture reshapes adult facial bone is not supported by good evidence. Described in Oscar Patel's public materials as compiled on 25 October 2026 from his website, videos, social posts and an unverified community document; the steps are a practical adaptation, not word-for-word instructions.","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC11793941/","is_group":false,"is_prenatal":false,"target_gender":["all"],"target_age_min":18,"sort_order":339},
{"id":"activity_oscar_slant_board_balancing","name":"Slant-Board Balancing","category":"Fitness","subcategory":"Posture and gait","domain":"fitness","type":"canonical","verb":"Balance","lane_subcategory":"Strength","is_active":true,"intensity":"low","description":"Standing on a low incline and shifting weight onto one leg.","duration_minutes":3,"default_duration_min":3,"steps":[{"order":1,"title":"Set up the board","instruction":"Place a low-incline slant board on the floor next to a wall or sturdy support."},{"order":2,"title":"Step on","instruction":"Stand on it holding the support."},{"order":3,"title":"Shift your weight","instruction":"Transfer your weight onto one leg."},{"order":4,"title":"Keep alignment","instruction":"Keep the ankle and knee in line over the foot."},{"order":5,"title":"Switch","instruction":"Change legs."}],"instructions":"1. Place a low-incline slant board on the floor next to a wall or sturdy support.\n2. Stand on it holding the support.\n3. Transfer your weight onto one leg.\n4. Keep the ankle and knee in line over the foot.\n5. Change legs.","safety_notes":"Always keep a hand on a support. Use a low incline and a non-slip board.","contraindications":["Acute injury, or pain that sharpens with the movement","Dizziness or balance problems (use a wall or chair for support)","Recent surgery, unless your clinician has cleared it","Ankle or Achilles injury"],"benefits":{"primary":"Trains single-leg balance and ankle alignment","secondary":["Calf mobility","Foot and ankle control"]},"health_benefits":["Trains single-leg balance and ankle alignment","Calf mobility","Foot and ankle control"],"tags":["oscar patel","balance","ankle"],"body_parts":["calves","legs","core"],"equipment_needed":["low-incline slant board"],"equipment":["low-incline slant board"],"recommended_duration":"Not specified by Oscar; 30 seconds per leg is a comfortable start","recommended_frequency":"Daily","recommended_intensity":"Comfortable and pain-free","tips":"Described in 