{"id":"activity_oscar_tongue_chewing","name":"Tongue Chewing","category":"Recovery","subcategory":"Tongue and swallowing","domain":"grooming","type":"canonical","verb":"Practice","lane_subcategory":"Jaw exercises","is_active":true,"intensity":"low","description":"Using the tongue to flatten and re-gather a small piece of gum against the palate.","duration_minutes":5,"default_duration_min":5,"steps":[{"order":1,"title":"Soften the gum","instruction":"Soften a small piece of sugar-free chewing gum."},{"order":2,"title":"Place it on the palate","instruction":"Position it against the roof of your mouth."},{"order":3,"title":"Flatten it","instruction":"Flatten it with the broad surface of your tongue."},{"order":4,"title":"Re-form it","instruction":"Gather it back into a small piece."},{"order":5,"title":"Repeat without clenching","instruction":"Repeat without clenching your teeth."}],"instructions":"1. Soften a small piece of sugar-free chewing gum.\n2. Position it against the roof of your mouth.\n3. Flatten it with the broad surface of your tongue.\n4. Gather it back into a small piece.\n5. Repeat without clenching your teeth.","safety_notes":"Use sugar-free gum and never clench the teeth. Stop if your jaw clicks, tires or hurts.","contraindications":["Dental work, braces or jaw (TMJ) pain: check with your dentist first","Difficulty swallowing or a choking risk","Oral injury, ulcers or infection","Under 18 unless a clinician has prescribed it","Chewing-gum or sugar-alcohol intolerance"],"benefits":{"primary":"Trains tongue strength and coordination","secondary":["Palate contact"]},"health_benefits":["Trains tongue strength and coordination","Palate contact"],"tags":["oscar patel","myofunctional","tongue","gum"],"body_parts":["mouth","tongue","jaw","lips"],"equipment_needed":["sugar-free chewing gum"],"equipment":["sugar-free chewing gum"],"recommended_duration":"5 minutes in the archived routine; some versions use 60 seconds × 2–3 sets","recommended_frequency":"Daily","recommended_intensity":"Comfortable and pain-free","tips":"Tongue and swallowing exercises taught by a clinician have trial evidence for sleep apnea (systematic review linked); the claim that tongue posture reshapes adult facial bone is not supported by good evidence. Described in Oscar Patel's public materials as compiled on 25 October 2026 from his website, videos, social posts and an unverified community document; the steps are a practical adaptation, not word-for-word instructions.","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC11793941/","is_group":false,"is_prenatal":false,"target_gender":["all"],"target_age_min":18,"sort_order":338},
